<h2>The arrest</h2>
<p>On 12 August 2025, police found the applicant inside a car at about 4 a.m. He told them he was a Delhi Police Sub-Inspector, but could produce no identity card of his own. A search, the prosecution said, yielded four Delhi Police identity cards, an SI uniform, a cap, a toy pistol and other items associated with police service. He was arrested the next day and remained in custody through the filing of the chargesheet in FIR No. 342/2025, Police Station Maurya Enclave.</p>

<h2>The competing narratives</h2>
<div class="compare">
  <div class="col old">
    <h3>The State</h3>
    <p>The paraphernalia pointed to impersonation with a purpose: the accused, it was argued, may have used the fake identity to cheat members of the public.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="arrow">&rarr;</div>
  <div class="col new">
    <h3>The defence</h3>
    <p>A false implication engineered by his brother-in-law, with whom he was locked in civil disputes — and, pointedly, not a single person alleging they were cheated.</p>
  </div>
</div>

<h2>Why &ldquo;may have cheated&rdquo; was not enough</h2>
<p>The Court&rsquo;s reasoning turned on what the investigation had and had not produced after fifteen months. It had produced no complainant: nobody alleged that the applicant extracted money, favours or compliance from them by posing as a police officer. It had not subjected the recovered identity cards — the central physical evidence of forgery — to forensic analysis. And the only items sent to the Forensic Science Laboratory, his laptop and phone, awaited a report that custody would not accelerate. The State&rsquo;s apprehension that the accused <em>may</em> have cheated people remained, on the record, an apprehension. Bail followed on a personal bond of Rs 20,000 with one surety.</p>

<h2>The principle beneath the facts</h2>
<p>Bail jurisprudence distinguishes between the gravity of what is alleged and the strength of what is shown. Impersonating a police officer is treated seriously — the offences under Sections 204 and 205 BNS protect public confidence in the uniform — but at the bail stage the question is whether continued custody is necessary: for investigation, to prevent tampering, or to secure presence at trial. Once the chargesheet is filed, the investigative justification largely drops away, and the court looks at what the collected material actually establishes. A case resting on possession, with the forensic link unbuilt and no victim identified, is a case in which detention pending trial does the work of punishment — which is not its function.</p>
<table class="law">
  <tr><th>Question at the bail stage</th><th>Answer on this record</th></tr>
  <tr><td>Is custody needed for investigation?</td><td>No — chargesheet filed; FSL report awaited regardless of custody</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Is there identified harm?</td><td>No complainant alleging cheating</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Is the physical evidence tested?</td><td>Identity cards not sent for forensic analysis</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Flight or tampering risk?</td><td>Addressed by bond, surety and trial-court supervision</td></tr>
</table>

<h2>Practice notes</h2>
<div class="check">
  <p><strong>Audit the forensic trail.</strong> In document and impersonation cases, check what was actually sent to the FSL and when. Untested exhibits are an argument, not a detail.</p>
  <p><strong>Absence of victims is substantive.</strong> Where the offence theory implies victims — cheating, extortion — their absence after a completed investigation speaks to the strength of the case.</p>
  <p><strong>Post-chargesheet custody needs fresh justification.</strong> Once investigation ends, the State must explain what detention still achieves; gravity alone does not answer that question.</p>
</div>
<div class="note">This note summarises a judgment of the Delhi High Court for general information and is not advice on any particular matter.</div>
